      {
        "id": "committee-work",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Where legislative detail is examined",
        "content": "The parliamentary rules reproduced by Syracuse University's Iran Data Portal describe specialized committees as working bodies that examine referred government bills and members' proposals. Their remit also includes investigating implementation, considering questions directed to ministers, and preparing reports. Committee activity thus connects legislative drafting with scrutiny of administration. [s2]\n\nThis distinction helps readers interpret parliamentary news. Referral to a committee, publication of a committee report, and approval by the full chamber describe different stages. A report may identify implementation problems or recommend an amendment without itself changing the law. The reproduced rules are useful evidence of procedural design, but their amendment history must be checked before presenting individual procedural details as current. [s2]"
        ,
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "representation-and-access",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Formal authority and political participation",
        "content": "Institutional powers alone do not explain who can exercise them. The House of Commons Library's March 2024 briefing describes candidate vetting by the Guardian Council and the exclusion of many reformist and moderate candidates. It also explains that parliamentary competition operates through factions and groupings rather than cohesive party blocs comparable to those found in some parliamentary democracies. These observations concern the political conditions of the 2024 election, rather than an assessment of every subsequent parliamentary decision. [s3]\n\nThe same briefing distinguishes Parliament from the Assembly of Experts, which has a separate leadership-selection function. Similar English translations of institutional names can obscure this difference: the Assembly of Experts is not another chamber through which ordinary parliamentary bills routinely pass. [s3]"
