    "seo": {
      "title": "Iranian Parliament: Membership and Powers | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "An introduction to Iran's Majles, its representative role, formal powers, and the documented outcome of the 2024 parliamentary election."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "chamber-and-membership",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the Majles is",
        "content": "The Islamic Consultative Assembly is commonly known as the Majles, a Persian term used for an assembly or council. The Inter-Parliamentary Union classifies Iran's parliament as unicameral: it has a single chamber rather than separate lower and upper houses. Its record for the 2024 election identifies 290 seats contested in a full renewal. [s1]\n\nThis institutional identity matters when reading references to other Iranian assemblies. Bodies involved in selecting the country's leadership or reviewing legislation have different functions; they should not be assumed to be additional elected parliamentary chambers. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "powers-and-responsibilities",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Legislation and executive scrutiny",
        "content": "The constitution gives Parliament powers to investigate national affairs and approve international agreements. It also provides for ministerial confidence votes and questioning. These are formal instruments of scrutiny; their existence does not establish how consistently they produce accountability. [s3]\n\nThe August 2024 cabinet appointment round offers a concrete example of parliamentary participation in executive formation: IRNA reported confidence votes approving the president's entire ministerial list. That event demonstrates the use of a constitutional procedure, rather than proving either parliamentary independence or executive control in every case. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "election-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading the 2024 election record",
        "content": "The IPU records voting between 1 March and 10 May 2024, encompassing the initial election and runoff. Its completed election record reports 14 women elected and a principlist majority. These are election-result observations, not a verified membership snapshot for October 2026. Vacancies and other later changes would require separate confirmation. [s1]\n\nThe March 2024 House of Commons Library briefing describes the restricted candidate field and the role of factions. This context is essential when interpreting an elected chamber: competition among approved candidates and access to candidacy are separate questions. Readers should also distinguish preliminary reporting from completed results, particularly when an election includes a runoff. [s2]"
        ,
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
